# The RPMC packets, Figure 50, p.76.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. See flash_controller_attached.espi.
#
# RPMC is Replay Protected Monotonic Counter. Table 16, p.81, marks both rows
# supported under Target Attached flash sharing and "No" under Controller
# Attached, which is why every command below is a PUT_FLASH_NP -- and it agrees
# with Table 5's Direction column, where both RPMC rows are Down only while
# every other flash row is Up/Down.
#
# THE RPMC PACKETS DO NOT LOOK LIKE THE OTHER FLASH PACKETS. Figure 48 gives
# Flash Read, Write and Erase a 7-byte header with a 32-bit address. Figure 50
# gives RPMC Op.1 and Op.2 a 3-byte header and no address at all. Table 16
# confirms it from the other direction: its Address Size column reads "N/A" for
# both RPMC rows and "4 B" for the other three. Reusing the flash request layout
# here would read four data bytes as an address and lose them.
#
# --- 1. RPMC Op.1, which carries data ---
#
#   0A     PUT_FLASH_NP                        Table 2, p.27
#   23     Cycle Type: 0 R1R0 00011 with R1R0 = 01b -> 2nd RPMC flash device
#
#          The encoding on p.49 is "0R1R0 6 00011" -- the raised 6 is a FOOTNOTE
#          MARKER sitting between R0 and the last five bits, and extraction
#          renders it as an ordinary digit. Eight bits, with R1R0 at [6:5], so
#          RPMC Op.1 spans 03h, 23h, 43h and 63h. Table 16 prints the same
#          encoding as "0R1R0 00011" on p.81, which is an independent second
#          reading of the worst cell in Table 5.
#
#          Note 6 heads its own table's column "P1P0" while the note text says
#          R1R0. That is a typo in the specification: note 1 already defines
#          P1P0 as the split completion field, and note 6's four rows describe
#          flash devices.
#
#   50     Tag 5, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   05     Length[7:0] = 05h -> 5 bytes       Table 16: "<= Max Payload Size"
#   9B     Data Byte 0, which Figure 50 labels "RPMC Opcode: OP1"
#
#          The value a target expects is advertised in configuration register
#          040h bits 31:24 for the 1st flash device, and in 048h and 04Ch for
#          the 2nd through 4th. The decoder names the byte and leaves the
#          comparison to a reader who has the configuration in front of them --
#          it is not in this packet.
#
#   01 02 03 04   the rest of the data
#   8A     CRC
#
# 03h is odd, so the cycle carries data, which is section 4.1.1's LSB rule on
# p.46 agreeing with Figure 50's Data brace.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 0A 23 50 05 9B 01 02 03 04 8A
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95

# --- 2. RPMC Op.2, which carries none ---
#
#   0A     PUT_FLASH_NP
#   04     Cycle Type: 0 R1R0 00100 with R1R0 = 00b -> 1st RPMC flash device
#   60     Tag 6, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   40     Length[7:0] = 40h -> 64 bytes requested
#
#          Table 16's Length column for the Op.2 row is "<= Max Read Request
#          Size", not payload size -- Op.2 asks for data rather than carrying
#          it, the same way a Flash Read does.
#
#   05     CRC
#
# Four bytes of command, and that is the whole packet: Figure 50 draws Flash
# RPMC OP2 as a 3-byte header with no Data brace at all. 04h is even, so the
# LSB rule says the same. Op.1 and Op.2 sitting on adjacent encodings with
# opposite data-carrying is the sharpest check on this pair of rows there is.
CMD 0A 04 60 40 05
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95

# --- 3. GET_FLASH_C returning the Op.2 completion ---
#
#   0B     GET_FLASH_C
#   31     CRC
#
#   08     ACCEPT
#   0F     Cycle Type: Successful Completion With Data, P1P0 = 11b
#   60     Tag 6, Length[11:8] = 0h            matches the Op.2 request
#   02     Length[7:0] = 02h -> 2 bytes
#   00 12  Data
#   0F 01  Status
#   58     CRC
#
# FIGURE 50 NAMES DATA BYTE 0 OF THIS PACKET "Extended Status" AND THE DECODE
# DOES NOT. The completion carries the ordinary Successful Completion With Data
# cycle type, shared with every flash read completion, so nothing on the wire
# says the request it answers was an RPMC Op.2. Naming the byte would take
# matching Tag 6 against the earlier transaction, which is transaction-layer
# state this decoder does not keep. Recorded rather than guessed -- the same
# call as leaving the GPIO expander direction as "configured per index".
CMD 0B 31
TAR
RSP 08 0F 60 02 00 12 0F 01 58
